[8] I remember when my grandmother first told me the legend of the River of Dreams, it was during one of those lazy afternoons when we'd sit by the window and watch the sun setting over the rolling hills. She said that long ago, before the town was even founded, the river had been known for its magical properties - anyone who had gone to wash in it reported having the most vivid and prophetic dreams. But the thing that truly fascinated her was the tales of people who had tried to harness the river's power, only to be driven mad by the intensity of the dreams that came to them. It was as if the river, in trying to share its secrets, had made them see too much, and too deeply.

I think this is where my interest in the river began<|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|><|im_end|>
