[8] I think my favorite short story ever came from a professor in college, Mr. Jenkins. He said it came from a fellow in the British Army during the Napoleonic Wars. It was called "The Last Card," and it was a perfect example of how a small, seemingly insignificant detail can change everything in a moment. The story went like this:

> 
> It was a cold and wet night in the Russian forest, and Private Jameson was sent to deliver a message to the commanding officer, who was camped deep in the woods. As he trudged through the knee-deep snow, his boots sank in, and his fingers started to freeze. But he pressed on, all the while keeping his eyes fixed on the card he carried, a rare and valuable playing card that his mother had given him before he left for the war.

> 
> Just as he reached the commanding officer's camp, a loud commotion erupted, and he saw a group of French soldiers approaching.
